==English==
[[File:ERBS.jpg|thumb|A spaceship]]

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|space ship}}

===Etymology===
* {{compound|en|space|ship}}
* {{sense|programming operator}} From its resemblance to the shape of a [[flying saucer]].

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|astronautics}} A [[vehicle]] that flies through [[space]].
# {{lb|en|cellular automata}} A [[finite]] [[pattern]] that reappears after a certain number of [[generation]]s in the same [[orientation]] but in a different [[position]].
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1989-10-26|author=Richard Schroeppel|title=Some recent Life results|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/J2llevRKwJI/m/qobp0C6TZCAJ|text=The '''spaceships''' look like a row of threshers harvesting a field of wheat; the thickness in the direction of motion is only about 10 cells.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Richard Nowakowski|title=More Games of No Chance|page=433|passage=We describe software that searches for '''spaceships''' in Conway's Game of Life and related two-dimensional cellular automata.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Andrew Adamatzky|title=Game of Life Cellular Automata|page=126|passage=Synthesis of '''spaceship''' flotillas is even more complicated than synthesis of oscillators, since spaceships are like oscillators that move{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} The [[operator]] <code><=></code> in certain [[programming language]]s, which compares two values and indicates whether the first is lesser than, greater than, or equal to the second.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Randal Schwartz; Brian Foy; Tom Phoenix|title=Intermediate Perl|page=142
|passage=If we reverse the positions of <code>$a</code> and <code>$b</code>, the '''spaceship''' will sort in the opposite order{{...}}}}

